\begin{lemma}[Infinite scalar ratio]
\label{lem:ancient-infinite-scalar-ratio}
For every $t_0\leq0$, $p$, and real $L,D$, there is $x$ with
\[
 d_{t_0}(p,x)>L,\qquad R(t_0,x)d_{t_0}(p,x)^2>D.
\]
\lean{PoincareMT.RicciFlow.infinite_scalar_ratio_of_bounded_ancient}
\uses{lem:ancient-positive-ratio-obstruction,lem:ancient-zero-ratio-obstruction}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If the scalar ratio were bounded at infinity, the positive-ratio obstruction
would force its limit superior to be zero. The zero-ratio rigidity theorem
would then make the slice flat. Bounded ancient Harnack propagates the
original positive scalar value to that slice, giving a contradiction.
\end{proof}

